## What is the DOM?

The **DOM**, or Document Object Model, is the browser's JavaScript representation of an HTML document. When the browser loads a page, it parses the HTML and creates a tree of objects that JavaScript can inspect and modify.

~~~diagram
{"type": "tree", "title": "HTML in, DOM objects out", "root": {"label": "document", "children": [{"label": "html", "children": [{"label": "body", "children": [{"label": "ul#tasks", "highlight": true, "children": [{"label": "li", "sub": "Buy milk"}, {"label": "li", "sub": "Walk dog"}]}, {"label": "button", "sub": "Add"}]}]}]}}
~~~

For example, this HTML:

~~~html
<body>
  <h1>My tasks</h1>
  <ul>
    <li>Learn JavaScript</li>
    <li>Build a React app</li>
  </ul>
</body>
~~~

becomes a structure containing elements such as `body`, `h1`, `ul`, and `li`.

JavaScript can then find those elements, change their content, add new elements, remove elements, and respond to user actions.

This is the foundation of traditional browser scripting. Later, React will provide a different way to describe UI updates, but understanding the DOM is still valuable because browsers, events, debugging tools, and React itself ultimately interact with the browser environment.

## Finding elements

The most common way to find an element is `querySelector()`.

It accepts a **CSS selector** and returns the first matching element.

~~~js
const heading = document.querySelector("h1");
const input = document.querySelector("#todo-input");
const button = document.querySelector("button");
~~~

An ID selector starts with `#`, a class selector starts with `.`, and an element selector uses the element name itself.

~~~js
const card = document.querySelector(".product-card");
const firstParagraph = document.querySelector("p");
~~~

If there is no matching element, `querySelector()` returns `null`.

~~~js
const missing = document.querySelector(".does-not-exist");
console.log(missing); // null
~~~

`querySelectorAll()` returns all matching elements.

~~~js
const tasks = document.querySelectorAll("li");
console.log(tasks.length);
~~~

The result is a **NodeList**, a collection of DOM nodes. You can use `for...of` with it.

~~~js
for (const task of tasks) {
  console.log(task.textContent);
}
~~~

You can also use `forEach()` on a NodeList in modern browsers.

~~~js
tasks.forEach(task => {
  console.log(task.textContent);
});
~~~

## Changing text

Once you have an element, you can change the text it contains using `textContent`.

~~~js
const heading = document.querySelector("h1");
heading.textContent = "My shopping list";
~~~

If the element contained existing child elements, `textContent` replaces the contents with text.

This makes `textContent` a good default when displaying text supplied by a user.

~~~js
const message = document.querySelector("#message");
message.textContent = userInput;
~~~

The browser treats the value as text rather than interpreting it as HTML.

## Changing attributes

HTML elements have **attributes**, which provide additional information about an element.

For example:

~~~html
<a id="documentation" href="https://developer.mozilla.org/">MDN</a>
~~~

JavaScript can read an attribute with `getAttribute()` and change it with `setAttribute()`.

~~~js
const link = document.querySelector("#documentation");

const currentUrl = link.getAttribute("href");
link.setAttribute("href", "https://react.dev/");
~~~

For common properties, you can often use the corresponding DOM property directly.

~~~js
link.href = "https://react.dev/";
~~~

You can also check whether an attribute exists:

~~~js
const input = document.querySelector("input");

if (input.hasAttribute("disabled")) {
  console.log("The input is disabled");
}
~~~

## Classes with classList

The `classList` property provides methods for working with CSS classes.

~~~js
const task = document.querySelector("li");

task.classList.add("done");
~~~

You can remove a class:

~~~js
task.classList.remove("done");
~~~

You can check whether a class exists:

~~~js
if (task.classList.contains("done")) {
  console.log("Task is complete");
}
~~~

And you can toggle a class:

~~~js
task.classList.toggle("done");
~~~

`toggle()` is particularly useful for interactive UI. Clicking a task can add the `done` class if it is missing and remove it if it is already present.

## Changing styles

You can modify inline styles through the `style` property.

~~~js
const heading = document.querySelector("h1");
heading.style.fontSize = "32px";
heading.style.marginBottom = "20px";
~~~

CSS property names that contain hyphens use camelCase in JavaScript.

~~~js
heading.style.backgroundColor = "lightgray";
~~~

For larger applications, it is usually cleaner to keep styling in CSS classes and use `classList` to change those classes rather than setting many individual inline styles.

For example:

~~~js
heading.classList.add("highlighted");
~~~

with CSS defining what `highlighted` means.

## Creating elements

You can create a new DOM element with `document.createElement()`.

~~~js
const listItem = document.createElement("li");
listItem.textContent = "Learn the DOM";
~~~

Creating the element does not automatically put it into the page. You need to append it to an existing element.

~~~js
const list = document.querySelector("#todo-list");
list.append(listItem);
~~~

Now the new `<li>` appears at the end of the list.

You can also create more complex structures by creating several elements and appending them to each other.

~~~js
const card = document.createElement("article");
const title = document.createElement("h2");

title.textContent = "Keyboard";
card.append(title);

document.body.append(card);
~~~

## Removing elements

An element can remove itself with `remove()`.

~~~js
const task = document.querySelector("li");
task.remove();
~~~

You can also remove children through their parent, although `remove()` is usually clearer when you already have the element.

## Events

An **event** is something that happens in the browser, such as a click, a keyboard action, text input, or form submission.

JavaScript can listen for events with `addEventListener()`.

~~~js
const button = document.querySelector("button");

button.addEventListener("click", () => {
  console.log("Button clicked");
});
~~~

The first argument is the event type. The second is the callback that the browser calls when that event occurs.

This is another example of functions being passed around as values.

## Common events

### click

The `click` event occurs when the user activates an element with a mouse, touch interaction, or keyboard in appropriate cases.

~~~js
button.addEventListener("click", () => {
  console.log("Clicked");
});
~~~

### input

The `input` event fires when the value of an input changes as the user types.

~~~js
const input = document.querySelector("#todo-input");

input.addEventListener("input", () => {
  console.log(input.value);
});
~~~

This is useful for live validation, search fields, and previews.

### submit

Forms have a `submit` event.

~~~js
const form = document.querySelector("#todo-form");

form.addEventListener("submit", event => {
  // Handle the form
});
~~~

The event fires when the user submits the form, including when they press Enter in an appropriate form control.

### keydown

The `keydown` event fires when a keyboard key is pressed.

~~~js
document.addEventListener("keydown", event => {
  console.log(event.key);
});
~~~

The event object tells you which key was pressed and provides other information about the event.

## The event object

When the browser calls your event handler, it provides an **event object**. This object contains information about what happened.

~~~js
button.addEventListener("click", event => {
  console.log(event.type);
  console.log(event.target);
});
~~~

`event.type` contains the event name, such as `click`.

`event.target` is the element that initiated the event.

For keyboard events, `event.key` tells you which key was pressed.

~~~js
document.addEventListener("keydown", event => {
  if (event.key === "Enter") {
    console.log("Enter pressed");
  }
});
~~~

## preventDefault

Some browser events have a default action. A form submission normally causes the browser to submit the form and potentially navigate or reload the page.

If JavaScript should handle the submission itself, call `preventDefault()`.

~~~js
form.addEventListener("submit", event => {
  event.preventDefault();

  console.log("Handling the form with JavaScript");
});
~~~

This tells the browser not to perform the event's default action.

The same idea is useful for links when you intentionally want to handle navigation yourself, although you should avoid disabling normal browser behaviour without a good reason.

## Event delegation

Suppose a to-do list contains many `<li>` elements. You could attach a separate click handler to every item.

~~~widget event-bubbling
~~~

~~~js
const items = document.querySelectorAll("#todo-list li");

items.forEach(item => {
  item.addEventListener("click", () => {
    item.classList.toggle("done");
  });
});
~~~

There is a problem: this only attaches handlers to items that exist when the code runs. If you add a new task later, it does not automatically receive the handler.

**Event delegation** solves this by attaching one handler to a parent element and checking which child triggered the event.

~~~js
const list = document.querySelector("#todo-list");

list.addEventListener("click", event => {
  if (event.target.matches("li")) {
    event.target.classList.toggle("done");
  }
});
~~~

The browser's event system allows the event to bubble from the target toward its ancestors. This is called **event bubbling**. Event delegation takes advantage of that behaviour.

If a list item contains nested elements, `event.target` may be the nested element rather than the `<li>`. In that case, `closest()` can be useful.

~~~js
list.addEventListener("click", event => {
  const item = event.target.closest("li");

  if (item && list.contains(item)) {
    item.classList.toggle("done");
  }
});
~~~

This pattern is especially useful for dynamic lists.

## Script placement and defer

JavaScript needs the DOM elements to exist before code tries to find them.

If a script is placed at the very beginning of the document, this can be a problem:

~~~html
<head>
  <script src="app.js"></script>
</head>
~~~

The script may execute before the browser has parsed the elements in the body.

One solution is to put the script near the end of the body.

~~~html
<body>
  <h1>My tasks</h1>

  <script src="app.js"></script>
</body>
~~~

Another common solution is the `defer` attribute.

~~~html
<head>
  <script src="app.js" defer></script>
</head>
~~~

A deferred external script is downloaded while the document is being parsed but executed after the document has been parsed. This allows your script to safely find elements in the page while still keeping the script in the document's head.

For modern applications, build tools and frameworks handle much of this for you, but understanding `defer` helps explain how ordinary browser scripts work.

## textContent versus innerHTML

There are two commonly confused ways to put content into an element.

`textContent` treats the value as plain text.

~~~js
message.textContent = "<strong>Hello</strong>";
~~~

The user sees the literal `<strong>Hello</strong>` text rather than a bold heading.

`innerHTML` interprets the value as HTML.

~~~js
message.innerHTML = "<strong>Hello</strong>";
~~~

Now the browser creates a `<strong>` element.

That can be useful when you intentionally generate trusted HTML, but it becomes dangerous when the HTML contains untrusted user input.

## The XSS risk

**XSS**, or Cross-Site Scripting, is a security problem where an attacker manages to inject executable content into a page that other users then load.

~~~compare
{"left": {"title": "innerHTML with user input", "lang": "js", "code": "li.innerHTML = task.title;\n// \"<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>\"", "note": "The browser runs whatever HTML the user typed: XSS."}, "right": {"title": "textContent", "lang": "js", "code": "li.textContent = task.title;\n// shown as plain text", "note": "Treated as text, never as markup."}}
~~~

For example, imagine a comment form where you take user input and assign it directly to `innerHTML`.

~~~js
message.innerHTML = userInput;
~~~

If `userInput` contains malicious HTML or script-related content, the browser may interpret it as markup rather than plain text. Depending on the context and browser protections, this can allow an attacker to execute unwanted code or manipulate the page.

For user-provided text, prefer `textContent`.

~~~js
message.textContent = userInput;
~~~

This is one of the most important rules to remember from this lesson:

> Treat user input as data, not HTML.

If you genuinely need to render HTML from an untrusted source, you need an appropriate sanitization strategy rather than blindly assigning it to `innerHTML`.

## Building a to-do list

Let's combine the main ideas into a small application.

Start with HTML containing a form, an input, an empty list, and a task counter.

~~~html
<form id="todo-form">
  <input id="todo-input" type="text" autocomplete="off">
  <button type="submit">Add task</button>
</form>

<ul id="todo-list"></ul>
<p id="todo-count">0 tasks</p>
~~~

JavaScript can then listen for form submission.

~~~js
const form = document.querySelector("#todo-form");
const input = document.querySelector("#todo-input");
const list = document.querySelector("#todo-list");
const count = document.querySelector("#todo-count");

form.addEventListener("submit", event => {
  event.preventDefault();

  const text = input.value.trim();

  if (text === "") {
    return;
  }

  const item = document.createElement("li");
  item.textContent = text;
  list.append(item);

  input.value = "";
});
~~~

You can then use event delegation to make every current and future list item clickable.

~~~js
list.addEventListener("click", event => {
  if (event.target.matches("li")) {
    event.target.classList.toggle("done");
  }
});
~~~

Finally, update the counter whenever the list changes.

~~~js
function updateCount() {
  const total = list.querySelectorAll("li").length;
  count.textContent = `${total} ${total === 1 ? "task" : "tasks"}`;
}
~~~

After adding an item, call `updateCount()`.

~~~js
list.append(item);
updateCount();
~~~

The same function could also be called after removing an item in a more advanced version.

## Common mistakes

### Forgetting preventDefault on forms

If you handle a form with JavaScript and forget `event.preventDefault()`, the browser may perform its normal submission behaviour, potentially navigating or reloading the page.

### Reading input.value too early

The value of an input changes as the user types. Read `input.value` when you actually need the current value.

### Adding listeners only to existing dynamic elements

If list items are created after the page loads, listeners attached only during initialisation will not automatically appear on new items. Event delegation on the parent list is often a simple solution.

### Using innerHTML with user input

Do not do this for untrusted text:

~~~js
todoItem.innerHTML = input.value;
~~~

Use `textContent` instead:

~~~js
todoItem.textContent = input.value;
~~~

### Assuming querySelector always finds something

This can fail:

~~~js
document.querySelector("#missing").textContent = "Hello";
~~~

If the element does not exist, `querySelector()` returns `null`. Make sure your selector matches the HTML and that the script runs after the relevant elements are available.

## From the DOM to React

Traditional DOM scripting asks JavaScript to find an element and directly change it. For example:

~~~js
title.textContent = "Updated title";
~~~

React takes a different approach. You describe what the UI should look like for a particular state, and React manages the DOM updates.

That does not make the DOM irrelevant. React event handlers still respond to browser events, and values such as `event.target` still come from the browser event system.

Understanding the DOM now gives you a useful foundation for understanding what React is abstracting away later. You will also be able to debug browser behaviour more confidently when something does not appear on the screen as expected.

For the rest of the course, keep one principle in mind: the browser is not just displaying static HTML. It is an interactive environment where JavaScript can respond to user actions and update the document safely.
